The skipped column is not decoration. A drifted anchor SKIPs -- neither SURVIVED
nor killed -- and mutate.sh's exit code reads only SURVIVED and UNSCORED, so an
all-skipped campaign exits 0 exactly like a clean sweep. Nine arms applied in
both runs; see clip-wire-workspace.README for the longer note.

Baseline was green before scoring in both runs and the identity control survived
both, so these verdicts are readable rather than noise. The lone remaining
survivor is that control, which is the required result.

WHICH TEST KILLED WHICH ARM, because a KILLED only says the SUITE went red and
this run added tests to a suite that already had 40. Each arm's log names the
failing assertion, so the attribution is read rather than inferred from arm
order -- three arms here fail the same test, and ordering alone could not tell
them apart. Repo memory attribute-kills-to-a-test-not-a-suite.

    enable      AnnouncesEveryEdit  'hiding a plane announces...' 2 != 1
    invert      AnnouncesEveryEdit  'inverting announces...' expected 4, was 3
    remove      AnnouncesEveryEdit  'removing announces...' expected 5, was 4
    refused     AnnouncesEveryEdit  'a REFUSED edit announces nothing' 11 != ...
    never shown AdvisoryIsShown     'the advisory strip is VISIBLE in the built
                                     tree, not merely decided to be'

Every one is an assertion written for that arm, in FlowVizClipPanelAnnounceTest.cpp
(GAP A, C) and its AdvisoryIsShown test (GAP B). No arm was killed by a
pre-existing test, and none was killed by a build error: all 40 enumerated tests
completed in all nine arms, so nothing was scored against a truncated session.

FIVE REAL SURVIVORS, AND NONE OF THEM WAS A DEFECT IN THE PANEL. Every one was
read against the source before anything was filed as work: the production code
did the right thing in all five cases. A SURVIVED arm says THE SUITE CANNOT
TELL. It never says the code is wrong, and acting on the first reading is how a
correct guard gets weakened to score a number -- see clip-wire-push.README for
that mistake's shape.

SO NOT ONE LINE OF SFlowVizClipPanel.cpp CHANGED to flip these five. The whole
close is tests, and that is the tell that the diagnosis was right: if closing a
survivor requires editing the code under test, either the arm found a real
defect or the arm is being made killable, and those two want very different
commits.

Tracked as task #59. All five gaps closed; see the after-verdicts above and the
CLOSED BY notes under each gap.

GAP A -- THE THREE ANNOUNCE ARMS (enable, invert, remove).

OnPlaneEnableClicked, OnPlaneInvertClicked and OnPlaneRemoveClicked each call
NotifyClipChanged() inside their .IsOk() guard, each with a comment saying why.
Nothing needs a broadcast added.

What makes the gap invisible from inside the suite is the arm right next to
them: "the preset handler announces nothing" WAS killed. So a test does prove
this panel can announce -- through exactly one control out of four. That single
green is what makes the other three look covered. The mechanism was verified
once and every other caller of it was assumed.

The test that closes this must observe THE DELEGATE, not the view model. All
three mutants leave the view model intact -- delete the NotifyClipChanged() call
and the plane still flips -- so a view-model assertion cannot kill them, and
writing one would close the task without closing the gap. Bind OnClipChanged to
a counter and assert the count moved.

A second trap in the same test: firing a handler on a panel with no subscriber
proves nothing either. ExecuteIfBound on an unbound delegate is the legal inert
state, so an unsubscribed panel passes a "did not crash" test and every mutant
survives it too.

CLOSED BY FlowViz.UI.ClipPanel.AnnouncesEveryEdit, which subscribes a counting
delegate and presses the enable, invert and remove buttons through the widget.
All three arms flipped to killed, each failing its own assertion. It opens with
a control arm -- the preset press, whose count MUST move -- because "the count
did not move" is the failure every arm below it looks for, and a panel that
dropped the OnClipChanged argument entirely would leave the counter at zero
forever and pass all four vacuously.

GAP B -- THE ADVISORY IS NEVER SHOWN. A different shape, and the more
interesting one.

The arm replaces the body of GetNotWiredAdvisoryVisibility with
`return EVisibility::Collapsed`, and it survives -- while "the advisory latches"
was killed. Both arms are about the same advisory, so the pair localises the
gap exactly: the PREDICATE (IsNotWiredAdvisoryVisible, an inline in the header)
is asserted directly, and the WIDGET-FACING function that consumes it is not.
The suite reads the predicate a user never sees and skips the value the layout
actually uses.

That is #54's own defect one layer down. #54 made the advisory retire correctly
when a volume binds; nothing checks it is ever DISPLAYED. A panel that never
shows it passes the suite today.

The fix is an assertion on GetNotWiredAdvisoryVisibility() itself -- Visible
when unbound, Collapsed when bound. Assert the exact enumerator rather than
"not Visible": Collapsed and Hidden differ, and the code says why (a Hidden
widget keeps its layout space, leaving a strip-sized hole once the advisory
retires).

CLOSED BY FlowViz.UI.ClipPanel.AdvisoryIsShown, which goes one step further than
the paragraph above proposed: it walks the built tree to the advisory BORDER and
reads the visibility the layout evaluated, rather than calling the accessor. The
accessor is private, and making it public to test it would have moved the same
indirection one step out without reaching what a user sees.

PUMPING IS NOT OPTIONAL THERE, and the trap points one way: an unpumped widget
returns its constructor's cached default, which for visibility is Visible. So
the VISIBLE assertion would pass on a widget with no binding at all, and the
COLLAPSED one is what carries the weight. The pair is what makes either mean
anything. See Tests/FlowVizSlateAttributePump.h.

GAP C -- THE REFUSED PRESET IS A DECISION, NOT OBVIOUSLY A GAP.

OnPresetClicked checks AddPresetPlane's result and announces only on success.
The arm announces unconditionally. What that costs is a redundant push of an
unchanged clip model -- a wasted flush for a frame that cannot have changed. It
is not a wrong image, and the panel is not broken with or without it.

So this one wants a decision rather than a reflex. Either assert it, or say in
the code that the looser behaviour is acceptable. What it should not do is sit
here as a survivor that the next reader has to re-derive.

Do NOT weaken the refusal itself to make the arm killable.

DECIDED: ASSERTED, in the last block of AnnouncesEveryEdit. The arm flipped to
killed. The refusal was not touched -- the assertion was added around it, which
is the only direction this was ever allowed to go.

Why assert rather than bless the looser behaviour: the gate is already written
and already correct, and an unasserted gate is one refactor away from being
deleted as dead weight. The cost of keeping it honest is four lines.

REACHING THE REFUSAL BRANCH TAKES SimulateClick, which BYPASSES the enabled
check, and that is deliberate. The button is disabled at the plane limit (rule
15), so a test that respected enablement could never reach the handler's refusal
branch at all: the guard would be unreachable and the assertion vacuous. Repo
memory unreachable-code-cannot-be-differentially-tested.

Arm 1 is the identity control and must SURVIVE. If it ever does not, the
harness is scoring noise and every line above is void.

The rationale lives here rather than at the top of the .txt because
parse_mutants.py splits on `%%` and raises ValueError on any record that is not
exactly three `--`-separated fields. A prose header would abort the campaign.
